1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:05,230
January 28, 1986, 73 seconds after liftoff,

2
00:00:05,610 --> 00:00:08,050
Space Shuttle Challenger came apart in the sky over Florida.

3
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:10,830
Seven people were on board.

4
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:14,270
Investigators spent months finding out why.

5
00:00:14,890 --> 00:00:16,730
The real answer started six hours earlier.

6
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:19,830
It started six hours earlier.

7
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:22,850
Thirty miles outside Brigham City,

8
00:00:23,690 --> 00:00:26,930
Utah, an engineer named Bob Ebeling is driving to work with his daughter.

9
00:00:26,930 --> 00:00:28,990
He pounds his fist on the dashboard.

10
00:00:30,510 --> 00:00:31,890
The challenger's going to blow up,

11
00:00:32,050 --> 00:00:33,790
he says. Everyone's going to die.

12
00:00:35,230 --> 00:00:37,570
He isn't guessing. He works for Morton Theacol,

13
00:00:37,970 --> 00:00:42,150
the company that builds part of the machine now sitting on a launch pad 600 miles away,

14
00:00:42,550 --> 00:00:46,190
hours from launch. Seven people are on that machine.

15
00:00:46,910 --> 00:00:48,110
One of them is a school teacher,

16
00:00:48,570 --> 00:00:52,530
chosen out of 11 ,000 applicants to be the first ordinary citizen in space.

17
00:00:53,790 --> 00:00:56,690
Bob Ebeling had spent the night before trying to stop this launch.

18
00:00:57,410 --> 00:01:00,010
This is the story of the warning nobody above him ever heard.

19
00:01:01,370 --> 00:01:03,310
This is the story of Space Shuttle Challenger.

20
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:06,430
The warning an engineer gave the night before launch,

21
00:01:06,910 --> 00:01:09,290
the eight degree reading nobody above him ever saw,

22
00:01:09,450 --> 00:01:11,370
and the 73 seconds that followed.

23
00:01:12,770 --> 00:01:14,170
Challenger was not new to this.

24
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:18,430
By January 1986, it had already flown to space nine times.

25
00:01:19,390 --> 00:01:21,930
It carried the first American woman into space,

26
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:24,470
the first American spacewalk by a woman,

27
00:01:24,930 --> 00:01:26,690
the first African -American astronaut.

28
00:01:27,390 --> 00:01:28,470
In its first three years,

29
00:01:28,610 --> 00:01:31,050
it flew more missions than any other orbiter in the fleet.

30
00:01:32,450 --> 00:01:35,630
This flight, designated STS -51L,

31
00:01:35,810 --> 00:01:38,810
had three jobs, deploy a communications satellite,

32
00:01:39,190 --> 00:01:41,810
release a small science satellite to study Halley's comet,

33
00:01:42,110 --> 00:01:44,710
and carry a passenger no shuttle had ever carried before.

34
00:01:44,710 --> 00:01:48,050
Not an astronaut, a teacher.

35
00:01:49,710 --> 00:01:53,130
In August 1984, President Reagan announced a new program,

36
00:01:53,630 --> 00:01:54,930
the Teacher in Space Project.

37
00:01:55,590 --> 00:01:58,150
The first ordinary citizen would fly on the space shuttle,

38
00:01:58,610 --> 00:02:00,670
and NASA would pick a school teacher to do it.

39
00:02:01,870 --> 00:02:03,390
11 ,000 teachers applied.

40
00:02:04,750 --> 00:02:06,270
The field narrowed to 114.

41
00:02:07,130 --> 00:02:10,850
Then, in July 1985, at a ceremony at the White House,

42
00:02:11,410 --> 00:02:15,110
NASA named its winner, Krista Ruddy's teacher,

43
00:02:15,330 --> 00:02:16,790
from Concord High School in New Hampshire.

44
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:22,370
She had written in her application that she wanted to demystify NASA and spaceflight

45
00:02:22,370 --> 00:02:24,430
to keep a journal in orbit that would,

46
00:02:24,750 --> 00:02:27,130
in her words, humanize the experience.

47
00:02:28,730 --> 00:02:31,710
She trained for months, zero gravity flights,

48
00:02:32,250 --> 00:02:36,370
simulators, meetings with the six professional astronauts she'd be flying alongside.

49
00:02:37,910 --> 00:02:42,090
Back home, her students at Concord High School planned to watch the launch live in the

50
00:02:42,090 --> 00:02:43,690
school auditorium on television.

51
00:02:45,450 --> 00:02:47,650
Six professional astronauts would fly with her.

52
00:02:49,270 --> 00:02:51,990
Commander Dick Scobee, a Vietnam combat pilot,

53
00:02:52,330 --> 00:02:54,070
flying his second mission into space,

54
00:02:54,490 --> 00:02:57,890
his first in command. Pilot Michael Smith,

55
00:02:58,410 --> 00:03:01,510
a Navy test pilot who had flown fighter jets off aircraft carriers.

56
00:03:02,010 --> 00:03:03,230
This was his first spaceflight.

57
00:03:04,570 --> 00:03:06,410
Mission Specialist Ellison Onizuka,

58
00:03:06,890 --> 00:03:08,510
an aerospace engineer from Hawaii,

59
00:03:08,970 --> 00:03:12,270
the first Asian -American to fly in space on his second mission.

60
00:03:13,910 --> 00:03:15,230
Mission Specialist Judith Resnick,

61
00:03:15,790 --> 00:03:18,370
an electrical engineer with a Ph .D.,

62
00:03:18,370 --> 00:03:20,470
one of the first American women to fly in space.

63
00:03:21,950 --> 00:03:23,470
Mission Specialist Ronald McNair,

64
00:03:23,870 --> 00:03:26,990
a physicist, a fifth -degree black belt on his second flight.

65
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:29,150
He had once played saxophone in orbit.

66
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:32,330
And Payload Specialist Gregory Jarvis,

67
00:03:32,890 --> 00:03:35,110
an engineer flying an experiment for his employer,

68
00:03:35,110 --> 00:03:38,970
bumped from three earlier flights before finally getting his seat on this one.

69
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:44,230
Seven people, and a machine with a part in it that had been quietly failing for nine

70
00:03:44,230 --> 00:03:49,270
years. The solid rocket boosters that lift the shuttle off the pad are built

71
00:03:49,270 --> 00:03:52,010
-in segments, sealed together with rubber O -rings,

72
00:03:52,290 --> 00:03:53,710
roughly the thickness of a rope.

73
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:58,730
In 1977, six years before Challenger ever flew,

74
00:03:59,350 --> 00:04:01,590
engineers testing those joints found something wrong.

75
00:04:01,590 --> 00:04:03,250
Under the pressure of ignition,

76
00:04:03,850 --> 00:04:06,550
the joint itself would bend and the seal could open a gap.

77
00:04:08,150 --> 00:04:09,410
They called it joint rotation.

78
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:12,350
It meant the O -ring wasn't just sealing a joint,

79
00:04:12,650 --> 00:04:15,810
it was fighting one. Four years later,

80
00:04:16,150 --> 00:04:17,790
on only the second shuttle flight,

81
00:04:18,250 --> 00:04:19,990
engineers found the first real evidence.

82
00:04:20,430 --> 00:04:22,850
An O -ring recovered after flight showed erosion.

83
00:04:23,450 --> 00:04:25,490
Something hot had been getting past the seal.

84
00:04:26,750 --> 00:04:30,570
Then, in January 1985, one year,

85
00:04:30,930 --> 00:04:32,890
almost to the day, before Challenger,

86
00:04:33,530 --> 00:04:35,530
a shuttle launched in the coldest weather yet.

87
00:04:36,390 --> 00:04:38,690
When investigators examined its boosters afterward,

88
00:04:39,090 --> 00:04:41,810
they found the worst O -ring damage anyone had ever seen.

89
00:04:43,210 --> 00:04:45,530
Cold weather and O -ring failure were no longer a theory.

90
00:04:46,170 --> 00:04:48,050
They were a pattern, sitting in a file,

91
00:04:48,410 --> 00:04:49,850
a full year before anyone died.

92
00:04:51,010 --> 00:04:56,170
On July 31, 1985, Theocol engineer Roger Boisigali put

93
00:04:56,170 --> 00:04:59,190
it in writing. If the joint problem wasn't fixed,

94
00:04:59,370 --> 00:05:01,130
he wrote, the result would be,

95
00:05:01,570 --> 00:05:03,810
quote, a catastrophe of the highest order,

96
00:05:04,310 --> 00:05:07,870
the loss of human life. Six months later,

97
00:05:08,050 --> 00:05:10,510
on the coldest morning a shuttle had ever launched in,

98
00:05:10,730 --> 00:05:12,650
that memo would still be sitting in a file.

99
00:05:14,150 --> 00:05:16,290
On the evening of January 27th,

100
00:05:16,370 --> 00:05:21,070
a forecast came in. Launch morning would be the coldest a shuttle had ever flown in.

101
00:05:21,710 --> 00:05:23,230
Someone had to decide if that mattered.

102
00:05:24,730 --> 00:05:27,910
A teleconference opened, connecting three places at once.

103
00:05:28,570 --> 00:05:29,870
Fiocol's engineers in Utah,

104
00:05:30,410 --> 00:05:31,650
NASA managers in Alabama,

105
00:05:32,050 --> 00:05:34,550
and NASA's team at Kennedy Space Center in Florida.

106
00:05:36,090 --> 00:05:37,970
Engineer Roger Boisigali presented the data.

107
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:40,130
Every time a shuttle had launched cold,

108
00:05:40,490 --> 00:05:41,810
its O -rings showed damage.

109
00:05:42,430 --> 00:05:44,950
The colder, the launch, the worse the damage.

110
00:05:45,630 --> 00:05:47,650
Fiocol's formal recommendation was blunt,

111
00:05:48,190 --> 00:05:49,990
do not launch below 53 degrees.

112
00:05:51,590 --> 00:05:54,710
53 degrees was the coldest they had ever flown in and survived.

113
00:05:55,490 --> 00:05:57,010
The forecast for the morning was eight.

114
00:05:58,650 --> 00:06:02,130
NASA pushed back. One manager said he was appalled by the recommendation.

115
00:06:02,790 --> 00:06:05,430
Another engineer would later recall a NASA official asking,

116
00:06:05,910 --> 00:06:09,230
in frustration, when exactly Theocol wanted him to launch,

117
00:06:09,650 --> 00:06:14,250
next April? Fiocol asked for a private caucus to talk it over,

118
00:06:14,650 --> 00:06:17,150
off the line. It was supposed to take five minutes.

119
00:06:17,570 --> 00:06:20,610
It took 30. Inside that room,

120
00:06:20,970 --> 00:06:24,650
a senior vice president turned to Fiocol's engineering chief and told him to take off

121
00:06:24,650 --> 00:06:27,310
his engineering hat and put on his management hat.

122
00:06:28,670 --> 00:06:31,250
When the line reopened, the recommendation had reversed.

123
00:06:32,170 --> 00:06:34,630
Fiocol's own management now said it was safe to fly.

124
00:06:35,510 --> 00:06:38,030
The engineers who had argued against it were not asked to vote.

125
00:06:39,370 --> 00:06:40,930
Roger Boycegley signed nothing.

126
00:06:41,430 --> 00:06:44,770
He later testified. I left the room feeling badly defeated.

127
00:06:45,310 --> 00:06:47,390
This was a meeting where the determination was to launch,

128
00:06:47,790 --> 00:06:50,530
and it was up to us to prove it was not safe to do so.

129
00:06:51,970 --> 00:06:55,930
One more Fiocol engineer at Kennedy Space Center refused to put his name on the launch

130
00:06:55,930 --> 00:06:57,990
approval. He told a NASA manager,

131
00:06:58,690 --> 00:07:00,090
if anything happens to this launch,

132
00:07:00,530 --> 00:07:02,470
I don't want to be the one explaining why we flew.

133
00:07:03,810 --> 00:07:06,950
At 11 .45 that night, someone else signed in his place.

134
00:07:07,510 --> 00:07:10,270
The recommendation to launch was telefax to Kennedy.

135
00:07:11,810 --> 00:07:13,510
The warning never went further than that room.

136
00:07:14,090 --> 00:07:17,490
It never reached the one NASA official whose job it was to give the final go.

137
00:07:18,870 --> 00:07:22,450
The next morning, ice crews walked the launch pad with broom handles,

138
00:07:22,870 --> 00:07:24,970
knocking icicles a foot long off the gantry.

139
00:07:26,450 --> 00:07:29,970
The overnight low had been the coldest Kennedy Space Center had recorded in years.

140
00:07:30,630 --> 00:07:32,830
The launch, already delayed for days,

141
00:07:33,310 --> 00:07:37,470
slipped another two hours while engineers argued over whether the ice itself was dangerous.

142
00:07:39,090 --> 00:07:41,650
An infrared scanner was pointed at the joint that mattered most.

143
00:07:42,310 --> 00:07:45,610
The same right side booster joint the engineers had warned about the night before.

144
00:07:47,070 --> 00:07:52,170
It read eight degrees. 45 degrees colder than the coldest launch the

145
00:07:52,170 --> 00:07:53,450
O -rings had ever survived.

146
00:07:54,030 --> 00:07:55,010
Nobody scrubbed the mission.

147
00:07:56,390 --> 00:07:58,410
At 11 .38 a .m. eastern time,

148
00:07:58,850 --> 00:08:00,690
Challenger lifted off pad 39B.

149
00:08:01,790 --> 00:08:03,890
Less than one second after ignition,

150
00:08:04,490 --> 00:08:05,730
cameras caught something small.

151
00:08:06,450 --> 00:08:10,210
A puff of black smoke right at the joint the engineers had warned about.

152
00:08:11,630 --> 00:08:13,530
The cold O -ring hadn't sealed properly.

153
00:08:14,150 --> 00:08:17,830
For a moment, burned grease and debris plugged the gap by accident.

154
00:08:19,150 --> 00:08:21,970
As the shuttle climbed, wine shear flexed the booster.

155
00:08:22,630 --> 00:08:25,090
The seal that had been accidentally plugged came open again.

156
00:08:26,430 --> 00:08:29,510
At 58 seconds, a small flame appeared at the joint.

157
00:08:29,510 --> 00:08:32,950
Invisible to the crew, barely visible on camera.

158
00:08:34,530 --> 00:08:36,690
At 65 seconds, the flame changed.

159
00:08:37,290 --> 00:08:41,450
It had burned through the wall of the external fuel tank and was now feeding on leaking

160
00:08:41,450 --> 00:08:46,410
hydrogen. At 68 seconds, mission control gave the standard call,

161
00:08:47,290 --> 00:08:48,650
Challenger, go with throttle up.

162
00:08:50,090 --> 00:08:51,410
Commander Dick Scobee answered,

163
00:08:52,170 --> 00:08:53,410
Roger, go with throttle up.

164
00:08:53,690 --> 00:08:55,510
It was the crew's last complete transmission.

165
00:08:56,770 --> 00:09:00,430
Four seconds later, pilot Michael Smith can be heard saying two words,

166
00:09:00,950 --> 00:09:06,130
a -oh. At 73 seconds, the flame burned through the strut holding

167
00:09:06,130 --> 00:09:07,350
the booster to the fuel tank.

168
00:09:07,910 --> 00:09:10,150
The tank ruptured, its contents ignited.

169
00:09:11,670 --> 00:09:14,310
The orbiter itself was never destroyed by an explosion.

170
00:09:14,970 --> 00:09:17,390
It was torn apart by the force of the air around it,

171
00:09:17,610 --> 00:09:19,590
moving at nearly twice the speed of sound.

172
00:09:21,070 --> 00:09:22,410
Seven people were on board.

173
00:09:23,650 --> 00:09:27,650
Six days later, President Reagan formed a commission to find out what happened.

174
00:09:28,130 --> 00:09:33,110
He gave it 120 days. Its chairman was a former secretary of state.

175
00:09:33,650 --> 00:09:35,190
Its vice chairman was Neil Armstrong.

176
00:09:35,910 --> 00:09:38,290
One of its members was a physicist named Richard Feynman.

177
00:09:39,630 --> 00:09:42,510
On live television, on February 11th,

178
00:09:42,510 --> 00:09:44,890
Feynman produced a small piece of O -ring material,

179
00:09:45,370 --> 00:09:47,730
clamped it, and dropped it into a glass of ice water.

180
00:09:49,150 --> 00:09:50,850
When he pulled it out and released the clamp,

181
00:09:50,850 --> 00:09:53,090
the rubber did not spring back into shape.

182
00:09:53,470 --> 00:09:56,190
It stayed flat, exactly as it had been forced.

183
00:09:57,790 --> 00:09:58,910
That was the whole failure,

184
00:09:59,270 --> 00:10:00,730
demonstrated in 30 seconds,

185
00:10:01,010 --> 00:10:03,350
in a glass of ice water in front of the country.

186
00:10:04,730 --> 00:10:08,410
The commission's technical finding matched what Boizegully had warned about six months

187
00:10:08,410 --> 00:10:11,190
earlier, almost exactly, a cold,

188
00:10:11,410 --> 00:10:15,250
brittle O -ring, in a joint that had never been designed to hold in the cold.

189
00:10:16,610 --> 00:10:17,870
But the commission went further.

190
00:10:18,510 --> 00:10:20,230
The real failure, it found,

191
00:10:20,710 --> 00:10:24,970
wasn't a part. It was a decision -making process it called clearly flawed,

192
00:10:25,630 --> 00:10:29,010
one where engineering warnings from the working level never reached the people making

193
00:10:29,010 --> 00:10:34,170
the final call. A sociologist who studied the case afterward gave that pattern

194
00:10:34,170 --> 00:10:36,530
a name, normalization of deviance.

195
00:10:37,130 --> 00:10:40,050
Each time a shuttle flew with damage and landed safely anyway,

196
00:10:40,610 --> 00:10:43,570
the danger looked a little more normal until the day it wasn't.

197
00:10:44,570 --> 00:10:48,610
For years afterward, one question about that day refused to go away.

198
00:10:49,270 --> 00:10:50,890
Did the crew survive the breakup itself?

199
00:10:52,590 --> 00:10:54,230
NASA investigated this specifically,

200
00:10:54,710 --> 00:10:57,310
and the answer it gave is more careful than most people expect.

201
00:10:58,710 --> 00:11:00,490
The crew compartment did not explode.

202
00:11:01,170 --> 00:11:05,690
It separated from the disintegrating orbiter largely intact and kept climbing for another

203
00:11:05,690 --> 00:11:07,650
25 seconds before it began to fall.

204
00:11:08,850 --> 00:11:12,410
It fell for two minutes and 45 seconds before it struck the ocean.

205
00:11:14,330 --> 00:11:17,430
Investigators later found that three of the four emergency air packs on board had been

206
00:11:17,430 --> 00:11:21,950
switched on by hand, evidence that at least some of the crew were alive and functioning

207
00:11:21,950 --> 00:11:26,970
after the breakup. NASA's own medical investigation concluded the

208
00:11:26,970 --> 00:11:28,690
forces of the breakup itself were,

209
00:11:29,270 --> 00:11:33,350
in its words, probably not sufficient to cause death or serious injury.

210
00:11:34,310 --> 00:11:37,710
Whether the crew stayed conscious for the fall is something the physical evidence could

211
00:11:37,710 --> 00:11:42,370
never fully answer. What NASA's investigators did state plainly,

212
00:11:43,110 --> 00:11:45,190
death is attributed to the impact with the ocean,

213
00:11:45,670 --> 00:11:48,970
not the breakup that came two minutes and 45 seconds before it.

214
00:11:50,190 --> 00:11:53,130
No one was ever criminally charged over the Challenger disaster,

215
00:11:53,750 --> 00:11:55,990
not at NASA, not at Theacol.

216
00:11:57,350 --> 00:11:59,950
The families were compensated through civil settlements instead.

217
00:12:00,550 --> 00:12:03,890
The government and Theacol jointly paid the families of four of the crew members.

218
00:12:05,070 --> 00:12:07,190
Three more families settled separately,

219
00:12:07,790 --> 00:12:11,710
directly with Theacol. The government did not contribute to those payments at all.

220
00:12:13,310 --> 00:12:15,930
Pilot Michael Smith's widow sued both the government and Theacol.

221
00:12:16,510 --> 00:12:19,090
A federal court dismissed her claim against the government outright.

222
00:12:19,850 --> 00:12:23,250
A legal doctrine bars lawsuits over a service member's death while on duty.

223
00:12:23,910 --> 00:12:26,490
Because Smith was Navy, that doctrine applied.

224
00:12:27,770 --> 00:12:31,450
The government that sent her husband to space never paid his family a dollar directly.

225
00:12:31,450 --> 00:12:33,230
Her claim against Theacol,

226
00:12:33,530 --> 00:12:35,910
as a private company, was allowed to proceed.

227
00:12:37,530 --> 00:12:41,770
Roger Boisgillie, the engineer who wrote the warning memo six months before the disaster,

228
00:12:42,210 --> 00:12:43,630
never worked on another rocket.

229
00:12:44,250 --> 00:12:49,110
He spent the rest of his career teaching engineering ethics at more than 300 universities

230
00:12:49,110 --> 00:12:54,190
and companies. In 1988, the American Association for the

231
00:12:54,190 --> 00:12:57,790
Advancement of Science gave him an award for scientific freedom and responsibility,

232
00:12:58,170 --> 00:13:00,490
for the warning almost no one had listened to.

233
00:13:01,730 --> 00:13:05,270
Bob Ebeling, the man who pounded the dashboard that morning,

234
00:13:05,850 --> 00:13:07,370
carried the guilt for 30 years,

235
00:13:07,910 --> 00:13:09,590
believing he could have stopped the launch himself.

236
00:13:11,170 --> 00:13:13,410
On the 30th anniversary, in 2016,

237
00:13:14,110 --> 00:13:17,250
he agreed to tell a reporter his story publicly for the first time.

238
00:13:18,030 --> 00:13:19,450
Listeners wrote him hundreds of letters.

239
00:13:19,870 --> 00:13:21,130
He died two months later.

240
00:13:22,570 --> 00:13:26,130
The one Theacol engineer at Kennedy who refused to sign the launch approval,

241
00:13:26,830 --> 00:13:30,370
Alan McDonald, later testified to the commission about the reversal that night,

242
00:13:30,370 --> 00:13:34,750
testimony that forced Theacol and NASA to admit what had actually happened in that room.

243
00:13:36,310 --> 00:13:37,490
All three men are gone now.

244
00:13:38,070 --> 00:13:39,670
None of them were ever blamed for what happened.

245
00:13:40,230 --> 00:13:41,250
Every one of them was right.

246
00:13:42,610 --> 00:13:44,490
The seven families didn't just grieve,

247
00:13:45,030 --> 00:13:48,710
they built something. Within a year of the disaster,

248
00:13:49,130 --> 00:13:51,810
they founded the Challenger Center for Space Science Education,

249
00:13:52,510 --> 00:13:56,350
a nonprofit designed to put kids through simulated space missions instead of letting

250
00:13:56,350 --> 00:13:59,050
the disaster be the last thing anyone remembered about it.

251
00:14:00,010 --> 00:14:03,250
Today, it runs 34 Challenger Learning Centers around the world,

252
00:14:04,810 --> 00:14:06,530
one of the newest opened in 2026,

253
00:14:07,210 --> 00:14:10,930
the first ever built from the ground up instead of retrofitted into an existing building.

254
00:14:11,670 --> 00:14:13,750
More than 800 people came to its opening.

255
00:14:15,390 --> 00:14:16,570
Commander Dick Scobee's widow,

256
00:14:17,150 --> 00:14:20,710
June Scobee Rogers, helped found the organization nearly 40 years ago.

257
00:14:21,310 --> 00:14:23,270
She still visits the centers in person today.

258
00:14:24,810 --> 00:14:27,490
Every January, NASA holds a day of remembrance.

259
00:14:28,020 --> 00:14:32,890
In 2026, family members came back to Kennedy Space Center for the 40th time.

260
00:14:34,370 --> 00:14:35,450
Pilot Michael Smith's widow,

261
00:14:36,010 --> 00:14:38,510
Jane Smith -Wolkot, stood at the memorial and spoke.

262
00:14:39,250 --> 00:14:41,130
Every day I missed Mike, she said.

263
00:14:41,590 --> 00:14:44,470
Every day's the same. His daughter,

264
00:14:44,570 --> 00:14:46,430
Alison, was 14 years old when he died.

265
00:14:46,970 --> 00:14:48,470
She spoke too, through tears.

266
00:14:49,330 --> 00:14:50,670
We are all part of this story,

267
00:14:50,830 --> 00:14:53,650
she said. 40 years later,

268
00:14:53,950 --> 00:14:56,130
they were still standing at the same launch complex,

269
00:14:56,130 --> 00:14:58,010
still saying his name out loud.

270
00:14:59,450 --> 00:15:01,830
The shuttle program was grounded for 32 months,

271
00:15:02,490 --> 00:15:04,350
the longest pause in its history.

272
00:15:05,770 --> 00:15:07,670
Engineers redesigned the joint completely,

273
00:15:08,070 --> 00:15:11,630
a third O -ring, a metal feature to physically lock the segments together,

274
00:15:11,870 --> 00:15:15,370
so the failure that killed seven people could not happen the same way twice.

275
00:15:16,990 --> 00:15:20,630
NASA created a new safety office that reported directly to the head of the agency,

276
00:15:21,150 --> 00:15:25,230
closing the exact gap that had kept a night before warning from ever reaching the person

277
00:15:25,230 --> 00:15:28,350
who needed to hear it. Decades later,

278
00:15:28,710 --> 00:15:31,170
when a different NASA spacecraft ran into trouble,

279
00:15:32,110 --> 00:15:34,130
astronauts stranded an unproven vehicle.

280
00:15:34,810 --> 00:15:36,450
The agency chose the slower,

281
00:15:36,830 --> 00:15:38,950
safer option instead of pushing the schedule.

282
00:15:39,650 --> 00:15:42,570
People who studied the decision point directly back to 1986.

283
00:15:44,170 --> 00:15:48,070
The warning that could have stopped Challenger existed in writing six months before the

284
00:15:48,070 --> 00:15:49,750
launch. It existed again,

285
00:15:50,170 --> 00:15:51,770
out loud, the night before.

286
00:15:51,770 --> 00:15:53,670
It never had to be a mystery.

287
00:15:54,190 --> 00:15:56,830
It just never reached the one person who could have said no.

288
00:15:58,250 --> 00:16:01,930
That's the part of this story that never gets old and never gets easier to hear.

289
00:16:03,390 --> 00:16:05,730
If a warning nobody heard is what got you about this one,

290
00:16:06,210 --> 00:16:08,870
wait until you see what nobody heard on Swiss Air Flight 111,

291
00:16:09,410 --> 00:16:12,750
a wiring fire in 17 minutes on this channel.

